      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "The relationship between bronchial hypersensitivity as the key phenomenon ofbronchial asthma and detoxication GSTM1 or GSTT1 gene polymorphism in children with neutrophilic phenotype of this disease remains unclear"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "33 children with bronchial asthma of neutrophile phenotype were examined in histamine and dosed physical exercise (running) tests. In addition GSTM1 and GSTT1 genotyping was performed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Histamine test revealed bronchial hypersensitivity (HTC < 0.3 mg/ml) in 30.7% of the children with GSTMI and/or GSTTI deletions versus 12.5% of those without deletions (P phi < 0.05). The relative risk ofhypersensitivity to histamine (HTC < 0.3 mg/mI) in the presence of deletions was estimnated as 3.1 (95% CI 1.4-6.4), at OR 2.4 (95% CI 1.08-3.1) andAR = 0.18."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Children with the neutrophilic phenotype of bronchial asthma having deletions in the GSTT1/GSTM1 system are characterized by bronchial hypersensitivity to histamine and dosed physical exercises."
        }
      ]},
